{%- comment -%}
  Prepended to every layout, so the assigns below are in scope for
  full / half_horizontal / half_vertical / quadrant.

  Merge data comes from src/transform.js:
    fault       non-empty when the poll or the account is the problem
    sensors     every sensor, in the order Ruuvi returns them
    featured    the same sensors in the installer's priority order
    unmatched   priority entries that matched no sensor
    updated_at  "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM" in the viewer's zone
    stats       { total, alarms, low_battery, stale }

  Layouts read `featured`, never `sensors` — that is what makes the priority
  field steer the small screens. `sensors` stays available for a layout that
  wants Ruuvi's own order.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Custom fields (see settings.yml) are consumed in src/transform.js, not here:
    ruuvi_token          authenticates the poll, via polling_headers
    featured_sensors     orders `featured`, and fills `unmatched`
    stale_after_minutes  decides is_stale, and so status
  `trmnlp lint` only scans markup and the polling settings for field names, so
  without this note it reports the last two as unused.
{%- endcomment -%}

<style>
  /* Everything the framework has a class for uses one: spacing, alignment,
     type, the grid, the corner radius, the header rule (border--h-1), icon
     display (inline-block). What is left is only what it has no utility for.

     Checked against plugins.css 3.3.0: `border--h-*` and `border--v-*` draw a
     single 1px edge, so there is nothing for a card's four-sided frame; there
     is no grid-auto-rows utility, so equal card rows need one declaration; and
     `data-clamp` is JavaScript with no CSS behind it, so the ledger's
     truncation needs a fallback for renderers where that has not run. */
  .ruuvi { gap: 0.75rem; block-size: 100%; }

  /* Equal rows, filling whatever height is left below the header. */
  .ruuvi__grid { grid-auto-rows: 1fr; flex: 1 1 auto; min-block-size: 0; }

  .ruuvi-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    border: 1.5px solid currentColor;
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }
  .ruuvi-card--primary { border-width: 3px; }
  /* A thick top edge marks a card that needs attention — legible from across
     the room, where a small chip is not. Replaces the Blade stylesheet's
     :has() rule; the condition is known in Liquid, so the class is just written. */
  .ruuvi-card--flagged { border-block-start-width: 8px; }

  .ruuvi-card__head { min-block-size: 1.25rem; }

  /* Name and hero, stacked, taking whatever height the stats and the footer
     leave — so those two stay pinned to the bottom of the card. Written out
     rather than assembled from flex utilities: `flex--col` centres its
     children where this wants them stretched, and the class that undoes that
     and the `portrait:` class below resolve by source order, not by intent. */
  .ruuvi-card__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* Where the slot is wider than it is tall, the two share a row instead and
     the card keeps a row's worth of height. The name takes the slack; the
     hero stops absorbing it and sits at the right. */
  .screen--portrait .ruuvi-card--wide .ruuvi-card__body { flex-direction: row; align-items: center; }
  .screen--portrait .ruuvi-card--wide .ruuvi-card__name { flex: 1 1 auto; }
  .screen--portrait .ruuvi-card--wide .ruuvi-card__hero { flex: 0 0 auto; }

  /* Icon, number and unit share a baseline; the row absorbs the slack so the
     stats and footer stay pinned to the bottom of the card. */
  .ruuvi-card__hero { align-items: baseline; flex: 1 1 auto; min-inline-size: 0; }

  /* The stats row is a hair from wrapping at text_scale large: the pair
     needs ~215px and a full-screen card gives 218, a quadrant's 207. Broken
     between the icon and its number it reads as two stacked rows, so the
     pair is kept on one line and allowed to use the padding instead. */
  .ruuvi-card__stats { border-block-start: 1.5px solid currentColor; }
  .ruuvi-card__stats > * { white-space: nowrap; }

  /* data-clamp is driven by the framework's JS. Truncating in CSS as well
     keeps a long room name on one line even where that script has not run.
     A wrapped name costs the row below it, and on a wide card it runs into
     the hero beside it — "Main bedroom north" at text_scale large is enough
     to do both. */
  .ruuvi-card__name,
  .ruuvi-ledger__name {
    display: block;
    min-inline-size: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The hero is what gives height back when a card is short; the name is a
     fixed line and must not be the one that shrinks, or `overflow: hidden`
     above clips it instead of ellipsising it. The wide rule higher up
     re-grants it the slack, along the other axis. */
  .ruuvi-card__name { flex: 0 0 auto; }

  /* Capping the ledger's column also buys back the width that ran "hPa" and
     "Time" together in the header. */
  .ruuvi-ledger__name { max-inline-size: 14ch; }

  .ruuvi-icon { vertical-align: middle; }
</style>

{%- assign primary = featured | first -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Staleness is drawn with the framework's gray text rather than opacity:
  opacity dithers badly on e-ink, and TRMNL's recipe review calls it out.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%} Centred message, used for both the fault and empty states. {%- endcomment -%}
{% template notice %}
  <div class="layout layout--col layout--center">
    <span class="value value--small">{{ headline }}</span>
    <span class="label">{{ detail }}</span>
  </div>
{% endtemplate %}

{%- comment -%}
  Small chips for a sensor's exceptional states. {% render %} is scope
  isolated, so the sensor has to be passed in explicitly.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% template sensor_flags %}
  {%- if sensor.alarm %}<span class="label label--small label--outline">{{ sensor.alarm }}</span>{% endif -%}
  {%- if sensor.battery_low %}<span class="label label--small label--outline">bat</span>{% endif -%}
  {%- if sensor.is_stale %}<span class="label label--small label--gray 1bit:text--black">stale</span>{% endif -%}
  {%- if sensor.voc_elevated %}<span class="label label--small label--outline">VOC {{ sensor.voc }}</span>{% endif -%}
{% endtemplate %}

{%- comment -%}
  One compact line: name on the left, readings on the right. Used by both
  half layouts, which differ only in how many lines fit and whether there is
  room for humidity.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% template sensor_line %}
  <div class="flex flex--row gap--small stretch-x{% if sensor.is_stale %} text--gray-30{% endif %}">
    <span class="label stretch-x" data-clamp="1">{{ sensor.name }}</span>
    {% render 'sensor_flags', sensor: sensor %}
    {%- if sensor.status == 'no_data' -%}
      <span class="value value--xsmall">&mdash;</span>
    {%- else -%}
      <span class="value value--small value--tnums">{{ sensor.temperature }}&deg;</span>
      {%- if with_humidity -%}
        <span class="value value--xsmall value--tnums">{{ sensor.humidity | default: '&mdash;' }}%</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{% endtemplate %}

{%- comment -%}
  One sensor as a two-line row: name and temperature on the first line, the
  humidity, the read time and any flags on the second. Used by the
  half-vertical below lg, which is about 375x450 — a tall, narrow slot where
  six of these read, and two columns of cards do not. sensor_line is the
  one-line version, for the half-horizontal, which is wide and short instead.

  Not the framework's `item` component, which this layout used to reach for
  and which needs `.meta` and `.content` around its parts — as bare children
  of `.item` they land in a flex row with `line-height: 0` and overlap. With
  the parts stacked correctly there is nothing of `item` left to want: an
  empty `.meta` is still drawn as a dotted bar on 1-bit, and an index in a
  10px column is not legible on a 375px half.

  `flex--col` centres its children rather than stretching them, and that has
  to be undone at every level or the temperature sits against the name
  instead of at the right edge: `stretch-x` fills the column this row is in,
  `flex--stretch-x` fills the row for the two lines inside it, and `stretch-x`
  on the name is what finally pushes the temperature right.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% template sensor_row %}
  <div class="flex flex--col gap--none stretch-x flex--stretch-x{% if sensor.is_stale %} text--gray-30{% endif %}">
    <div class="flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--xsmall">
      <span class="title title--small stretch-x" data-clamp="1">{{ sensor.name }}</span>
      {%- if sensor.status == 'no_data' -%}
        <span class="value value--small">&mdash;</span>
      {%- else -%}
        <span class="value value--small value--tnums">{{ sensor.temperature }}&deg;C</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--xsmall">
      {%- if sensor.status == 'no_data' -%}
        <span class="label label--small stretch-x">awaiting reading</span>
      {%- else -%}
        <span class="label label--small stretch-x">
          {{- sensor.humidity | default: '&mdash;' }}% &middot; {{ sensor.measured_at -}}
        </span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {% render 'sensor_flags', sensor: sensor %}
    </div>
  </div>
{% endtemplate %}

{%- comment -%}
  Lucide icons (v1.16.0, ISC), inlined because a plugin cannot reference local
  assets. Sized by the caller; `currentColor` keeps them in step with the text
  around them, including the gray a stale card is drawn in.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% template icon %}
  {%- assign px = size | default: 24 -%}
  <svg class="ruuvi-icon inline-block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="{{ px }}" height="{{ px }}"
       viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
    {%- case name -%}
      {%- when 'thermometer' -%}
        <path d="M14 4v10.54a4 4 0 1 1-4 0V4a2 2 0 0 1 4 0Z" />
      {%- when 'thermometer-sun' -%}
        <path d="M12 2v2" /><path d="M12 8a4 4 0 0 0-1.645 7.647" /><path d="M2 12h2" />
        <path d="M20 14.54a4 4 0 1 1-4 0V4a2 2 0 0 1 4 0z" />
        <path d="m4.93 4.93 1.41 1.41" /><path d="m6.34 17.66-1.41 1.41" />
      {%- when 'thermometer-snowflake' -%}
        <path d="m10 20-1.25-2.5L6 18" /><path d="M10 4 8.75 6.5 6 6" /><path d="M10.585 15H10" />
        <path d="M2 12h6.5L10 9" /><path d="M20 14.54a4 4 0 1 1-4 0V4a2 2 0 0 1 4 0z" />
        <path d="m4 10 1.5 2L4 14" /><path d="m7 21 3-6-1.5-3" /><path d="m7 3 3 6h2" />
      {%- when 'battery-full' -%}
        <path d="M10 10v4" /><path d="M14 10v4" /><path d="M22 14v-4" /><path d="M6 10v4" />
        <rect x="2" y="6" width="16" height="12" rx="2" />
      {%- when 'battery-medium' -%}
        <path d="M10 14v-4" /><path d="M22 14v-4" /><path d="M6 14v-4" />
        <rect x="2" y="6" width="16" height="12" rx="2" />
      {%- when 'battery-low' -%}
        <path d="M22 14v-4" /><path d="M6 14v-4" />
        <rect x="2" y="6" width="16" height="12" rx="2" />
      {%- when 'battery' -%}
        <path d="M22 14v-4" /><rect x="2" y="6" width="16" height="12" rx="2" />
      {%- when 'droplet' -%}
        <path d="M12 22a7 7 0 0 0 7-7c0-2-1-3.9-3-5.5s-3.5-4-4-6.5c-.5 2.5-2 4.9-4 6.5C6 11.1 5 13 5 15a7 7 0 0 0 7 7z" />
      {%- when 'gauge' -%}
        <path d="m12 14 4-4" /><path d="M3.34 19a10 10 0 1 1 17.32 0" />
      {%- when 'wind' -%}
        <path d="M12.8 19.6A2 2 0 1 0 14 16H2" /><path d="M17.5 8a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 2 4H2" />
        <path d="M9.8 4.4A2 2 0 1 1 11 8H2" />
      {%- when 'clock' -%}
        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" /><path d="M12 6v6l4 2" />
      {%- when 'triangle-alert' -%}
        <path d="m21.73 18-8-14a2 2 0 0 0-3.48 0l-8 14A2 2 0 0 0 4 21h16a2 2 0 0 0 1.73-3" />
        <path d="M12 9v4" /><path d="M12 17h.01" />
    {%- endcase -%}
  </svg>
{% endtemplate %}

{%- comment -%}
  One sensor as a card, for the large-screen grid in full.liquid.

  Sized for a ~240x190 box. That is what the device actually gives: it reports
  1872x1404, but renders at scale_factor 1.8, so the CSS canvas is ~1040x780
  and a 4-column grid leaves about 200px of content width per card.

  Pass `wide: true` where a portrait screen leaves the slot wider than it is
  tall. A half-vertical on a portrait X is 375x1010, so a column of six cards
  gives each one ~155px of height for a card that wants ~175 — and the read
  time ran off the bottom edge. With `wide` the name moves up beside the hero
  in portrait, a row the card no longer has to pay for. The quadrant takes it
  for the same reason. The full screen does not: its portrait cards are
  ~245x230, tall enough to stack and too narrow to share a row.

  The flagged modifier replaces the stylesheet's `:has()` rule from the Blade
  original — the condition is known here, so the class can just be written.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% template sensor_card %}
  <article class="ruuvi-card py--3 px--4 rounded--small
      {%- if rank == 1 %} ruuvi-card--primary{% endif -%}
      {%- if wide %} ruuvi-card--wide{% endif -%}
      {%- if sensor.alarm or sensor.battery_low %} ruuvi-card--flagged{% endif -%}
      {%- if sensor.is_stale %} text--gray-30{% endif -%}">
    {%- comment -%}
      The temperature icon rides in the head rather than beside the number,
      which the hero row needs the width for.

      Hero size is set by the widest reading, not by taste. value--tnums is
      tabular, so the minus and the decimal point are full digit widths:
      "-21.2" is five 0.6em glyphs, not three digits and two narrow marks.
      At value--large (58px) that is ~174px inside a ~204px card, and
      text_scale: large then overruns it — which is exactly what a freezer
      tag did on the device, twice. value--base (38px) needs ~114px and
      leaves room to spare.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--xsmall ruuvi-card__head">
      <span class="label label--small label--gray 1bit:text--black">{{ rank_label }}</span>
      {% render 'icon', name: sensor.temp_icon, size: 16 %}
      <span class="stretch-x"></span>
      {%- if sensor.alarm -%}
        <span class="label label--small label--error">{% render 'icon', name: 'triangle-alert', size: 12 %} {{ sensor.alarm }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if sensor.is_stale -%}
        <span class="label label--small label--gray-out">{% render 'icon', name: 'clock', size: 12 %} stale</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Only a Ruuvi Air can raise this, and only when the index says the air
        is worth doing something about — so the card stays quiet the rest of
        the time, which is most of it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if sensor.voc_elevated -%}
        <span class="label label--small label--warning">VOC {{ sensor.voc }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {% render 'icon', name: sensor.battery_icon, size: 18 %}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Name and hero. Stacked by default; side by side where `wide` says the
      card is wider than it is tall, which is the whole of the modifier. The
      wrapper is what carries that, so the hero still absorbs the card's
      slack and the stats and footer stay pinned to the bottom.

      The name is clamped to one line either way: "Living room freezer" is 19
      characters and a real name, and a second line would cost the stats row
      its space. The unit sits beside the number rather than above it —
      stacked, it cost 24px of height the card does not have.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if sensor.status == 'no_data' -%}
      <div class="ruuvi-card__body">
        <span class="title title--small ruuvi-card__name" data-clamp="1">{{ sensor.name }}</span>
        <div class="flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--xsmall ruuvi-card__hero">
          <span class="value value--base">&mdash;</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <span class="label label--small label--gray 1bit:text--black">awaiting reading</span>
    {%- else -%}
      <div class="ruuvi-card__body">
        <span class="title title--small ruuvi-card__name" data-clamp="1">{{ sensor.name }}</span>
        <div class="flex flex--row gap--xsmall ruuvi-card__hero">
          <span class="value value--base value--tnums">{{ sensor.temperature }}</span>
          <span class="value value--xsmall">&deg;C</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        A Ruuvi Air shows CO2 where a RuuviTag shows pressure. Pressure is the
        same number on every card in one house — it is the least informative
        value on the screen — and CO2 is the reason the Air is there.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="flex flex--row flex--between flex--center-y pt--2 ruuvi-card__stats">
        <span class="value value--small value--tnums">
          {% render 'icon', name: 'droplet', size: 12 %} {{ sensor.humidity | default: '&mdash;' }}%
        </span>
        {%- if sensor.co2 != blank -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            No unit, because no other stat carries one: humidity is a droplet
            and a number, pressure a gauge and a number. The icon is the unit
            marker here, and spelling out "ppm" cost the row enough width to
            wrap both stats onto second lines once text_scale had its share.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="value value--small value--tnums">
            {% render 'icon', name: 'wind', size: 12 %} {{ sensor.co2 }}
          </span>
        {%- else -%}
          <span class="value value--small value--tnums">
            {% render 'icon', name: 'gauge', size: 12 %} {{ sensor.pressure_hpa | default: '&mdash;' }}
          </span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      <span class="label label--small label--gray 1bit:text--black">
        {%- if sensor.co2 != blank %}PM2.5 {{ sensor.pm25 | default: '&mdash;' }} &middot; {% endif -%}
        read {{ sensor.measured_at }}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </article>
{% endtemplate %}
